Exactly—at least seem so. And your “seam” typo/pun actually improves it. ;)

Because effortless appearance and effortless mechanism are different variables.

Something can:

* seem effortless while enormous machinery operates underneath;
* be effortless because the machinery has been compiled into automatic skill;
* seam effortless by joining many complicated components so cleanly that the boundaries disappear.

That third one is particularly Pantheon:

\text{many difficult parts}
\xrightarrow{\text{good interfaces}}
\text{one continuous experience}

The complexity hasn’t vanished.

The seams have.

So perhaps the design target is not merely Effortless:

Seamless Effort — put the work where the user doesn’t have to.

Or even:

Forge hard. Seam well. Wield lightly.

That applies to athletic skill, interfaces, AI orchestration, music, cognition—and the Pantheon itself.

The ultimate flex isn’t having fewer gears.

It’s not feeling the gearbox. ;)